            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is generally air tight however there are a few large air leakage locations::



** The hole below the main floor bathtub (as seen from the basement ceiling) - needs to be sealed from the attic



** The hole around the plumbing stack (as seen from the basement ceiling) - needs to be sealed from the attic



** The old chimney "chase" that goes from the basement up through the roof - needs to be sealed from the attic



** The attic access panel can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as "Draft Attack"



** There is a 5" fresh air supply connected to the return air ductwork of the furnace. This pipe should have a manual "in-line" damper so you can adjust the amount of fresh air your house needs.



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 91 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. If you complete most of the air sealing efforts in the list above, it is highly likely the reduction in air leakage will become 20% or higher, doubling the energy savings &amp; increasing the rebate to $750.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 with no additional exterior insulation. You indicated that you would like to replace the current siding on your house and are interested in adding more wall insulation at the same time. Current housing built in Yellowknife today would have a minimum R30 wall insulation, so I modeled adding another R20 to the exterior of your house walls (behind the new siding) in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). 



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 676 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



NOTE: As the pony wall section of your basement has the same exterior siding, you will be carrying this additional R20 insulation down over approximately 50% of the foundation walls &amp; this will qualify for further rebates. Please see the Foundation section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The basement walls have a lower concrete section that is insulated from the interior and an upper pony wall section that is constructed similar to the upper main floor walls, with the same exterior siding. The right side of the basement is insulated with fibreglass batt insulation, while the left side has been spray foamed. In conjunction with your main floor exterior insulation (adding R20) &amp; new siding project, the new insulation &amp; siding will transition down over the portion of the basement walls that has the same siding (approximately 50% of the basement wall area).



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to the exterior of the foundation walls (the section that currently has siding &amp; includes the basement 2x10 header space between the foundation &amp; main floor), you could potentially save $ 277 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,125 in rebates from the DHERP. In addition to the $8,000 for the main floor walls, this now totals up to $9,125 towards the R20 exterior insulation !!



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house is generally air tight and includes a 5" fresh air supply pipe (connected to the return air plenum of the furnace) in order to provide "make up" air each time an exhaust fan (kitchen range hood, bathrooms &amp; clothes dryer) is operating. The house will become more air tight once you address some of those pesky air leaks outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report.



If this house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. Retrofitting an HRV into an existing house is most commonly done by connecting the supply &amp; return air connections into the furnace ductwork. However, in your case, the house is very close to being well balanced from an air tightness perspective ............. so you could get by with operating your "spot ventilation" (range hood &amp; 1 or 2 of the bathrooms) a little longer - perhaps with a timer or humidity sensing switch.



If you decide to install an HRV, please consider installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) that is a minimum 70% efficient in transferring heat from the outgoing air to the incoming fresh air. The furnace can still be used to circulate the incoming "pre-heated" air throughout the house.



Savings:

By installing a 70% efficient ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, this will increase ventilation that will offset some of the savings from reducing the air leakage locations listed previously in my report, however recovering 70% of the heat from the outgoing air is way better than losing 100% of the heat from the air leakage locations !! There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for installing an HRV. </VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $10,625 in rebates (perhaps $17,500)  from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $1,125 for the basement insulation

     - $   500 for the air leakage reduction ($750 if you achieve a 20% reduction)

     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, 3 x bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.1 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace &amp; hot water tank are direct vent appliances are not susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - There are three bathroom exhaust fans

        - There is a 5" fresh air supply connected to the "return air" ductwork of the furnace



There is a 5" fresh air supply connected to the return air ductwork of the furnace that is meant to provide fresh air to the house &amp; "make-up" air for when other exhaust fans are operating (ie: range hood, bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer).



In the "Ventilation" section of this report, I highlight that your house may become slightly under-ventilated after you address those pesky air leakage locations we identified. If your house was being built today, it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed. If you choose not to install an HRV, you could increase the "exhaust only" ventilation (with additional make-up air from the 5" fresh air supply) by installing a timer or humidity sensor switch on the main floor bathroom fan.



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fans with ENERGY STAR rated fans that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



